+# P31 adversarial review and Nikolskii lemma
+
+Work in progress; the final version will replace this checkpoint.
+
+## Errors identified at the first audit
+
+**PROVED.** The principal displayed P31 inequality has no error in its Fejér–Riesz/averaging derivation, provided its Nikolskii polynomial is **analytic**, with frequencies `0,...,L`. The phrase “every trigonometric polynomial of degree <= L” is incorrect under the ordinary two-sided convention. Smallest counterexample to that equivalence: `L=1`, `U=1+2 cos x`; its fourth/second norm ratio is `19/9 > (L+1)rho_L=3/2`.
+
+**PROVED.** A bound `rho_L <= 0.95` for **all** `L>=0` is impossible: `rho_0=1`. The nontrivial uniform range must start at `L=1`.
+
+**PROVED.** P31(iii) drops a factor `1+epsilon`. At a fixed scale the derivation gives a threshold `2 delta/[rho(1+epsilon)]`, not `2 delta/rho`. This distinction is material at `epsilon=1`; details and exact finite formulas follow below.
+
+**PROVED.** The P29 corollary as printed needs the positive part: `(n-X/2)_+^2/(2X)`. Without it, `A={12}` has `M=1` but the asserted lower bound is `25/24`. This is the smallest counterexample by maximum frequency: when `n<X/2` and `X<=11`, the printed bound is at most `(1-X/2)^2/(2X)<1`, whereas every nonempty `A` has `M>=1/2` (a sharper small-case check will be supplied if a global minimality claim is needed). The singleton counterexample is sufficient to invalidate the unqualified statement; no minimality over all sets is currently claimed.
+
+## Current frontier
+
+The main P31 inequality survives. Remaining tasks are a full explicit Nikolskii bound, careful finite/asymptotic corollaries, VM tests on the requested adversarial families, and a source check. No numerical experiment has yet been run for this report. No claim is kernel-checked.

