The corrected P31 argument proves square-root bounds for every fixed density
\[
\delta>\frac{2}{3\sqrt3}\approx0.384900.
\]
At a fixed window scale, the front-loading threshold retains the factor \(1+\varepsilon\); the smaller-scale threshold follows only by taking \(\varepsilon\) arbitrarily small.

For the sparse-tail question, I proved a bound independent of the largest frequency: an interval core \([1,Y]\) plus \(r\) tail frequencies has \(M\ge 2Y/(3\pi)-r\). I also checked the Fejér low-pass approach. It removes the tail exactly, but turns a full core into a weighted polynomial with minimum at least \(-1/2\), so that smoothing route loses the negative dip it needs to detect.
